#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "669126b0-2995-549c-9a80-888bccd3acc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21845" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[-5,5).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/17/178be718fdba262f895193f9c503667f4ba3f709db23717893016d500ba4bc8b.png)\nSince $f$ is ![](images/6c/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\nand  ![](images/6c/6c274adc750119956ce950c5212283e01dfddf37137178bf0b85295a6837f433.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/9d/9d14fd1a227fc073b09d78684cb8dbff507bf083da988dda2755270a77981455.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6}\n+ \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5),$\n\n- continuous on $[-5,5),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5),$ if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3.$\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous} }$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-3) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{107}{6})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{250(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-50(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{50(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221201 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/id #uuid "6e45e60d-7c68-5c49-8ca2-5381af39cd47" :question/math-academy-id "e-21846" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9b34c017e88b9ed4f80f45223c5093a2f934d249d666b3c1d929718bc05e9882.png)\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x \\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}.$\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n- $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2).$\n\n- $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/5c/5c1cc57825a318df088ac0bc28ffe75c21579c5fe007fbb52cf0710a1937dc1e.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f5fb59869184a0c54ddaeea0b434104f3335c652c3f7533cd9dec0dcdcc91876.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221202 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "77c71e9d-c2a4-5a2c-9c67-a90e8913ba13" :question/math-academy-id "e-21856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[-4,4).$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n![](images/7d/7ddcc5e5d8580428341aec84ff2982c593054ec7ec363cc11139d87912dab58d.png)\n Since $f$ is \n![](images/91/91f9e216819e2259c6cc4ce19c39e38f8d75c9a5902019de9e437e0732477943.png)\non $\\mathbb{R},$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n![](images/1c/1c6cf5957b07d2564607d603276b7fb9cffbe7502f53c6ea77a10b726d95aefd.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n \\geq 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2}\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-4+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4}))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{1}{4}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{[-4t]}_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{3} & ∼-4x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{192(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317343 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021c5018-8f8c-5e1b-90f6-fedc8773f310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315126} {:answer/id #uuid "b4412b0a-c71f-5cca-bfd7-0e7a394cd882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316137} {:answer/id #uuid "d4107e3e-ce7b-572c-a7b0-63308ed4b323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "021c5018-8f8c-5e1b-90f6-fedc8773f310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315126} :answer-field/id #uuid "384932b1-045f-5e97-adf9-8c7d788221a0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316679} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdd7d9d-de70-5aed-afa2-c306cd50b6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315462} {:answer/id #uuid "969ebb27-77fb-5bb9-b065-1e365c811b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315955} {:answer/id #uuid "a3599bbd-841c-52c8-ba53-48371a212d90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "969ebb27-77fb-5bb9-b065-1e365c811b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315955} :answer-field/id #uuid "766fd31b-a411-57ed-afac-05d8e91addc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ee338f-35b8-53aa-9cc0-9a42b626d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315264} {:answer/id #uuid "46c2a83e-e1b4-587f-8352-258685818859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315530} {:answer/id #uuid "f1bb71dc-c7a5-5888-9c5c-a0c6c35f5aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316463}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ee338f-35b8-53aa-9cc0-9a42b626d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315264} :answer-field/id #uuid "b496164e-826b-5d5a-bf37-8bb4bcd3b015" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316971} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a95f78-0264-56af-b724-e87bacdc6ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315590} {:answer/id #uuid "6db3446f-e7d6-5cee-aa7f-d50b7a128c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315740} {:answer/id #uuid "90039fb4-359d-54c4-a7d0-6cad1bc614a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db3446f-e7d6-5cee-aa7f-d50b7a128c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315740} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c4e885-4811-5e1e-84d1-0bbc46d06da5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "33a3c9fc-2528-5adc-9b2e-4d84493c2477" :question/math-academy-id "q-342949" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x+1, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because $\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/77/7736417b068541a1609efa46e9824cd3f65cd34856c1d2f5d0ec7258ec883b5c.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ is not piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ because ${F}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}(2x+1)=2(-3)+1=-5\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and II are false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/ac/ac4d89fcccca99bebaeab4b44baac9c5c03651c38cef7e4d14cfe57978d066b7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221450 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102c20a7-364b-5e1d-8e5b-1a8f7adb1463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315216} {:answer/id #uuid "8f4a5b80-b993-5acb-b1fc-0027ff1b76db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315921} {:answer/id #uuid "9bfc4594-abfa-5c41-b497-cf307852b689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315989} {:answer/id #uuid "9f833301-cf56-51d2-aeca-49f450beeb21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :ans