d #uuid "779b25e7-e9f1-5970-81e6-c2457d778bb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315791} {:answer/id #uuid "bb973c70-a9f5-5ed1-9595-2ee7f36dd4eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316179} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc1e478-54e9-577d-b817-e82797475d03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316357} {:answer/id #uuid "ea83f06e-8df8-5e52-bb5b-19418d0ece66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316422}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "731d5dd7-66a6-50bd-b943-5e3d2433a22a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315769} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1a1345d-5208-544e-96f2-59511c4c5a07" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce2d6842-c273-5f28-8b8b-7bdf00107d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-343245" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension of $G(x)=-14x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}+4∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}}\nand\n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-14x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${a}_{0}$ and ${a}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-17+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-3,3),$\n\n- continuous on $[-3,3),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-3)=F(3),$ if we extend the value at $x=3$ using the formula $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{continuous}$ and $\\text{piecewise smooth}$ on $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-17)+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -14x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{252(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-84(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ -14x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{84(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221451 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e10f17d-3081-5b2d-b4a9-a342b2c3c345" :answer/type {:d